data.content:
Here's the updated code:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
    {
        _timeProvider = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;
    }

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**

- `TimeProvider` is built into .NET 8+ — no NuGet needed for production code.
- The `= null` default means existing callers don't break (`new InvoiceCalculator()` still works).
- No DI container required.

**In tests**, install one package and use `FakeTimeProvider`:

```
dotnet add <TestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(365));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 6, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Apply the same pattern to your other classes that read `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`. If your project targets .NET 6/7, add the `Microsoft.Bcl.TimeProvider` NuGet to get the same `TimeProvider` API.
